Example 20: molecules and vacancies together on multi-nary target sites
=======================================================================

Physical system
---------------
A hybrid organic-inorganic perovskite, (MA,FA)Pb(I,Br)3, with Schottky
vacancies on both sublattices.  The A site is shared by two molecular cations
-- methylammonium (MA = CH3NH3+) and formamidinium (FA = HC(NH2)2+) -- and by
A-site vacancies; the X site is shared by iodide, bromide and halide vacancies.

What it exercises
-----------------
Both targets are multi-nary (nk = 2, i.e. two substituent species plus the
remaining species) and both mix all three kinds of occupancy that genersod
knows about:

  Target 1, the A site (8 sites):   @FA  %VA  @MA
      1 FA molecule, 1 vacancy, 6 MA molecules.
      Note the *remaining* species is itself a molecule.

  Target 2, the X site (24 sites):  Br   %VX  I
      1 Br, 1 vacancy, 22 I -- plain atoms either side of a vacancy.

Until version 0.90 genersod refused this input outright: molecule (@) and
vacancy (%) prefixes were only honoured on binary targets, and any @ or % on a
target with nk >= 2 aborted at startup.  The generic site resolver introduced
in 0.91 handles them on any target and in any slot, and this example is the
regression test for that.

Files
-----
  INSOD          SOD input (2x2x2 supercell, 2 targets, nk = 2 each, FILER = 11)
  SGO            Symmetry operations for Pm-3m (space group 221)
  MA.xyz         Methylammonium geometry (8 atoms: C, N, 6 H)
  FA.xyz         Formamidinium geometry (9 atoms: C, 2 N, 6 H)
  EQMATRIX       Site-equivalence matrix written by combsod
  OPERATORS      Symmetry operators written by combsod
  supercell.cif  2x2x2 CsPbI3 supercell in P1 (40 sites)
  n01_01_01_01/ENSEMBLE   The 114 inequivalent configurations

How to run
----------
   ../../bin/sod_comb.sh      # enumerate -> n01_01_01_01/ENSEMBLE
   ../../bin/sod_gener.sh     # materialise -> n01_01_01_01/c*/POSCAR

Expected output
---------------
114 inequivalent configurations, each written as a VASP POSCAR of 88 atoms:

  C   7     1 from FA + 6 from MA
  H  42     6 from FA + 36 from MA
  N   8     2 from FA + 6 from MA
  Pb  8     the untouched B site
  Br  1
  I  22

The two vacancies contribute no atoms, which is why the 40 parent sites give
88 atoms rather than the 96 that eight fully occupied molecular A sites and 24
occupied X sites would produce.  Molecule orientations are sampled at random,
so coordinates differ from run to run but the composition above does not.
